  "slug": "condensation-dynamics-e624",
  "summary": "Recent preprints use polymer models to recover chromosome ensembles and dynamics from contact and epigenomic data, extending beyond static contact maps.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the use of polymer models to infer chromosome organization and dynamics from experimental readouts. An epigenetic-state-informed polymer model generates conformational ensembles that reproduce Hi-C maps and reveal folding landscapes across genetic and epigenetic contexts. A Full-Inversion Chromatin model instead learns an effective force field from Hi-C contacts to infer budding yeast chromosome ensembles and locus-dependent dynamics across the cell cycle. What is new in these studies is the recovery of unobserved dynamical quantities, such as locus-level dynamics and force fields, from static structural data. A ground-truth polymer simulation study cautions that thresholding noisy 3D distances from microscopy misestimates looping probability, lifetime, and search time. The MIST single-molecule tracking preprint is a methodological neighbor rather than a chromatin study.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-12"
}
